      <workList xml:id="workDesc_2447142c">
         <work xml:id="work_id811bb2b6">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_d8ed0b43">C1:011</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id2da9bc44" xml:lang="de">Über die musikalische Composition</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_a76b2b7f"/>
            <history xml:id="history_idbb888edb">
               <p xml:id="idm206">In 1771 Scheibe advertised in the newspaper <rend xml:id="idm207" fontstyle="italic">Kiøbenhavns</rend>
                  <rend xml:id="idm208" fontstyle="italic">Adresse-Contoirs Efterretninger</rend> that, encouraged by friends, he intended to establish a collegium giving lectures in music on subjects ranging from its nature, genres and styles to taste, and furthermore provide lessons in musical composition. The collegium began in August, and Scheibe apparently made a careful planning and produced texts for the lectures. He decided, however, to write a complete work on musical composition and to have it published employing the texts as a basis for the book. A good friend of his in Leipzig found a cheap publisher, and the first part of the treatise which Scheibe had finished was printed in 1773. The printed part of the treatise deals with intervals, harmony and chords as well as harmonic progressions, major and minor keys and church modes, metre and time signatures, and finally also solmisation. It includes an extensive introduction which not only summarises the subjects of the treatise but is in parts also autobiographical and retrospective. He mentions his previous publications as well as discussions with <persName xml:id="idm209" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName> and the disagreements with <persName xml:id="idm210" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Lorenz Christoph Mizler</persName>, in addition to the genesis of the present work. He also includes biographical information on some of the musicians and composers at the beginning of the eighteenth century. Finally, Scheibe mentions that he will have the remaining parts finished within two years. The second part which remained in manuscript is an incomplete working manuscript including numerous cancellations and additions in ink, prepared by Scheibe just before 1776. The second part consists of four chapters of which the first is an introduction dealing with melodic syntax, and the other parts are concerned with intervals and intervallic progression. Scheibe includes numerous examples which in particular are copied from Italian vocal music. Soon after Scheibe’s death in 1776 the manuscript was acquired by The Royal Library. The two remaining parts which Scheibe promised in his first book were never completed. Sometime between the publication of the treatise and his death in 1776, Scheibe sent a copy (untrimmed and possible unbound) to the famous <persName xml:id="idm211" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Giovanni Battista Martini</persName> in Bologna. It seems likely that Martini asked for a copy. On the title page Scheibe wrote in his characteristic hand 'Al molto Reverendo Padre Martini, a Bologna.' Among the Martini collection is also an incomplete translation of Scheibe's preface (pp. iii–lii), most likely carried out for Padre Martini by an Italian-speaking German. Four years after the publication of <rend xml:id="idm212" fontstyle="italic">Über die musikalische Composition</rend> – that is, the year after Scheibe had passed away – a positive critique of the first part appeared in <rend xml:id="idm213" fontstyle="italic">Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek </rend>in which the reviewer, <persName xml:id="idm214" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Christian Gottlob Neefe</persName>, was looking forward to seeing the remaining parts of the useful work.</p>
            </history>
